 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, assuming efficient connected component labeling (e.g., Union-Find or BFS/DFS) [G]. Otsu’s method is also $O(N)$ [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the binary mask, label map, and component counts [G].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; effectively removes small noise artifacts; computationally efficient [G].
-*   **Limitations:** Assumes the target object is the largest connected region in the thresholded volume [G]. Fails if the target is fragmented or if noise forms a larger connected component than the target [G]. Does not handle intensity variations within the object [G].
-*   **When to Use:** Post-processing step for binary masks where the object of interest is expected to be a single large contiguous blob [G].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When the object is fragmented, or when intensity-based thresholding is insufficient to separate the object from background noise of similar intensity [G].
+**Time Complexity:** Dominated by Otsu thresholding ($O(N)$) and Connected Component Labeling ($O(N \alpha(N))$ where $\alpha$ is inverse Ackermann function).
+**Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for binary mask, labels, and areas.
+**Strengths:** Automatic thresholding removes manual parameter tuning. Largest component selection effectively filters noise.
+**Limitations:** Relies on Otsu's assumption of bimodal histogram, which may fail in complex medical data. Connectivity definition (4 vs 8/26) significantly impacts results.
+**When to use:** Spine segmentation in medical volumes where the spine is the dominant high-intensity structure.
 
 ## Background
-This method is described in the context of GPU-based visualization of high-resolution medical images, specifically for spine segmentation [S1, unofficial]. It combines Otsu’s thresholding with connected component analysis to isolate the spine from other anatomical structures in CT volumes [S1, unofficial]. The approach is part of a broader pipeline for medical volume rendering [S1, unofficial].
+Originated in medical image processing for spine segmentation. Cited in [S1, unofficial] as part of a GPU-based visualization pipeline.
 
